      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The city’s name comes from the Yokuts word chaushila, honoring the indigenous Chowchilla band that once lived along the river.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Chowchilla,_California",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Chowchilla lies 16 miles northwest of Madera on California Route 99, right in the heart of the Central Valley.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chowchilla%2C_California",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "route_only"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Two state prisons—Central California Women’s Facility and Valley State Prison—are both located within Chowchilla’s limits.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chowchilla%2C_California",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In the early 1900s, a 12‑mile narrow‑gauge railroad was built to support Chowchilla’s agricultural boom, though it was later abandoned.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
